**5. `CertGenUtilsTests.java:240-246` — the "duplicates and whitespaces are ignored" check never actually checks what it claims.**
```java
assertThat(keyUsage.hasUsages(KEY_USAGE_MAPPINGS.get("digitalSignature")), is(true));
assertThat(keyUsage.hasUsages(KEY_USAGE_MAPPINGS.get("nonRepudiation")), is(true));
assertThat(keyUsage.hasUsages(KEY_USAGE_MAPPINGS.get("digitalSignature")), is(true));  // duplicate of the first, line ~244
assertThat(keyUsage.hasUsages(KEY_USAGE_MAPPINGS.get("keyEncipherment")), is(true));
```
The third assertion repeats the first. Failure scenario for a regression: if `buildKeyUsage` were changed to keep only the first occurrence of a name (or to drop whitespace-trimmed duplicates), every assertion in this block would still pass, because each pair of inputs is a distinct name — the block cannot distinguish dedup/whitespace handling from a plain loop, so the test provides no protection for the behavior it claims to verify.
